Description: A simple and unified evaluation toolkit for benchmarking single-cell
    and multiomics data simulation methods. Evaluates how realistic simulated data
    are compared to real biological datasets across three multiomics experimental
    designs: (i) paired multiomics (simultaneous co-assay from identical cells,
    e.g. 10x Chromium Multiome, SHARE-seq, SNARE-seq), which unlocks the full
    Category 7 cross-modal coupling suite including FOSCTTM alignment scores and
    peak-to-gene regulatory linkage; (ii) unpaired multiomics (independent scRNA-seq
    and scATAC-seq profiled from separate cells of the same tissue), supported
    through per-modality unimodal evaluation (Categories 1-6, 8) and population-level
    cross-modal metrics such as co-expression module fidelity and network Jaccard
    similarity; and (iii) mosaic multiomics (partial co-measurement where only a
    subset of cells has both modalities, e.g. DOGMA-seq or mosaic pooling designs),
    handled by subsetting the paired cell fraction for coupling metrics and evaluating
    each modality independently otherwise. The toolkit quantifies simulation realism
    using 48 curated, non-redundant evaluation metrics (39 unimodal, 48 multiomics)
    organized into 8 canonical categories:
    (1) Distribution Differences: Statistical distances between real and simulated values (such as KS, Wasserstein, and MAD),
    (2) Summary Properties: Gene correlations, zero counts, and dropout patterns,
    (3) Cell Structure and Clustering: How well distinct cell types and clusters are preserved (such as Silhouette width, ARI, and NMI),
    (4) Batch and Confounder Mixing: How well technical batch variations are captured or mixed,
    (5) Biological Signals and Marker Genes: Preservation of differentially expressed genes (DEGs) across Simpipe, SimBench, and Shaky Foundations frameworks,
    (6) Cell Lineage and Trajectories: Preservation of continuous developmental pathways (inferred directly from scRNA-seq counts),
    (7) Multiomics Relationships: Connections between different layers (such as gene expression and chromatin accessibility) — fully active for paired data; population-level metrics available for unpaired and mosaic data,
    (8) Computational Resources: Speed (runtime in seconds) and memory usage (peak RAM in MiB).
    Includes non-linear low-dimensional cell embedding projections (UMAP, t-SNE, PCA) with quantitative cluster separability (Silhouette) and fidelity (ARI) metrics, automated score normalization, leaderboards, 600 DPI bubble plots, and an interactive Shiny app studio.
